        <body>
  <h2>Student Rights & Discipline in Central Islip UFSD: A Parent's Guide to Manifestation Determinations, Suspensions, and Due Process</h2>

  <p>The Central Islip Union Free School District (UFSD) is committed to providing a safe and supportive learning environment for all students. As part of this commitment, students have rights and responsibilities that are clearly defined in the district's Code of Conduct. This document outlines expectations for student behavior and the disciplinary consequences that may result from violations. However, for students with disabilities, the disciplinary process is governed by federal and state laws designed to protect their rights and ensure fair treatment. This guide will walk you through the critical aspects of student discipline in Central Islip UFSD, focusing on Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), suspensions, and your due process rights under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Education Law.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Central Islip UFSD Code of Conduct</h3>

  <p>The Central Islip UFSD Code of Conduct is the primary document outlining acceptable and unacceptable student behaviors, along with the consequences for violations. As a parent, it's crucial to obtain and thoroughly review this document.  You can typically find it on the district's website or by contacting the school administration.  Pay close attention to the following:</p>

  <ul>
      <li><b>Definitions of offenses:</b> Ensure you understand the specific behaviors that are considered violations.</li>
      <li><b>Disciplinary procedures:</b>  Understand the steps the school will take when addressing student misconduct, including investigation, notification, and hearings.</li>
      <li><b>Range of consequences:</b>  Familiarize yourself with the potential disciplinary actions, ranging from warnings and detention to suspensions and expulsion.</li>
      <li><b>Student rights:</b> The Code of Conduct should also outline students' rights during disciplinary proceedings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): Protecting Students with Disabilities</h3>

  <p>For students with disabilities who have an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a 504 plan, the disciplinary process differs significantly from that of their non-disabled peers. Federal law, specifically the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), mandates a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) when a disciplinary action involves a suspension exceeding 10 school days (either consecutive or cumulative within a school year) or a change in placement. 34 CFR § 300.530.</p>

  <p><b>What is a Manifestation Determination Review?</b> An MDR is a process to determine if the student's behavior that led to the disciplinary action was a manifestation of their disability. In other words, did the student's disability cause or significantly contribute to the behavior?  This review is conducted by the IEP team, including the parent(s), and must occur within 10 school days of the decision to change the student's placement because of a violation of a code of student conduct. 34 CFR § 300.530(e).</p>

  <p><b>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide</b></p>

  <ol>
      <li><b>Notification:</b> The school must notify you, as the parent, of the decision to suspend your child for more than 10 days or to change their placement due to a disciplinary infraction. This notification should clearly state the reason for the disciplinary action and inform you of your right to participate in the MDR.</li>
      <li><b>Information Gathering:</b> The IEP team reviews all relevant information in the student’s file, including:
        <ul>
          <li>The student's IEP</li>
          <li>Evaluations and re-evaluations</li>
          <li>Information provided by the parents</li>
          <li>Teacher observations</li>
          <li>Relevant medical information</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><b>The MDR Meeting:</b> The IEP team convenes to discuss whether the behavior was a manifestation of the student's disability. To make this determination, the team must answer two key questions (34 CFR § 300.530(e)(1)):
        <ul>
          <li>Was the conduct in question caused by or directly and substantially related to the child's disability?</li>
          <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP?</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><b>Determination and Outcomes:</b> The IEP team makes a determination based on the information reviewed. There are two possible outcomes:
        <ul>
          <li><b>The behavior *is* a manifestation:</b> If either of the questions above are answered "yes," the behavior is considered a manifestation of the student's disability. In this case, the IEP team must:
            <ul>
              <li>Conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted.</li>
              <li>Develop and implement a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) if one does not already exist, or revise the existing BIP if necessary.</li>
              <li>Return the student to their original placement, unless the parents and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the BIP.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li><b>The behavior is *not* a manifestation:</b> If both questions are answered "no," the behavior is not considered a manifestation of the student's disability. In this case, disciplinary actions can proceed as they would for a non-disabled student. However, the school district still has to provide services to the student during the period of suspension, as described below.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Important Considerations for the MDR Meeting:</b></p>
  <ul>
      <li><b>Prepare thoroughly:</b> Review your child's IEP, evaluations, and any relevant documentation. Prepare a statement outlining your perspective on the incident and how you believe your child's disability may have contributed to the behavior.</li>
      <li><b>Bring an advocate:</b> Consider bringing a special education advocate or attorney to the MDR meeting to support you and ensure your child's rights are protected.</li>
      <li><b>Ask clarifying questions:</b> Don't hesitate to ask questions about the information presented and the reasoning behind the team's decision.</li>
      <li><b>Document everything:</b> Keep detailed records of all communication and decisions made during the MDR process.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Services During Suspension: Ensuring Continued Educational Progress</h3>

  <p>Even when a student with a disability is suspended for more than 10 days, the school district is still responsible for providing services to ensure they can continue to participate in the general education curriculum and progress toward their IEP goals. (34 CFR § 300.530(d)). These services can include:</p>
  <ul>
      <li>Instruction in academic subjects</li>
      <li>Behavioral support services</li>
      <li>Related services, such as speech therapy or counseling</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The specific services provided will vary depending on the student's individual needs and the length of the suspension. It's crucial to discuss these services with the school district and ensure they are appropriate for your child.</p>

  <h3>Challenging Disciplinary Decisions: Your Due Process Rights</h3>

  <p>If you disagree with the outcome of the MDR, the disciplinary action taken, or the services being provided during suspension, you have the right to challenge the school district's decision through due process. IDEA provides parents with several procedural safeguards, including the right to (34 CFR § 300.500 et seq.):</p>

  <ul>
      <li><b>Mediation:</b> A voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the school district reach a mutually agreeable resolution.</li>
      <li><b>Due Process Complaint:</b> A formal complaint filed with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) alleging a violation of IDEA.</li>
      <li><b>Impartial Hearing:</b> A hearing conducted by an impartial hearing officer (IHO) who will review the evidence and make a decision on the dispute.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Filing a Due Process Complaint in New York State:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Meet the Filing Deadline:</b> In New York, you generally have two years from the date you knew or should have known about the alleged violation to file a due process complaint.
        <li><b>Content of the Complaint:</b> The due process complaint must be specific and detailed, including:
            <ul>
                <li>The name and address of the child.</li>
                <li>The name of the school the child attends.</li>
                <li>A description of the nature of the problem, including the facts relating to the problem.</li>
                <li>A proposed resolution of the problem to the extent known and available to the parent at the time.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><b>Model Form:</b> NYSED provides a model due process complaint form on its website, which can be helpful in ensuring your complaint includes all the necessary information.</li>
        <li><b>Resolution Meeting:</b> Within 15 days of receiving the due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution meeting with the parents and relevant members of the IEP team. The purpose of this meeting is to attempt to resolve the dispute.</li>
        <li><b>Hearing Timeline:</b> If the dispute is not resolved at the resolution meeting, the case proceeds to an impartial hearing. The IHO typically issues a decision within 45 days of the expiration of the 30-day resolution period (or from the conclusion of the resolution meeting if an agreement is not reached).</li>
    </ol>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tips for Disciplinary Matters:</b></p>

  <ul>
      <li><b>Stay informed:</b> Understand your child's rights under IDEA and New York State Education Law.</li>
      <li><b>Communicate effectively:</b> Maintain open and respectful communication with school officials.</li>
      <li><b>Document everything:</b> Keep detailed records of all communication, meetings, and decisions.</li>
      <li><b>Seek expert assistance:</b> Don't hesitate to seek assistance from a special education advocate or attorney.</li>
      <li><b>Focus on solutions:</b> Work collaboratively with the school to develop strategies to address your child's behavior and prevent future disciplinary issues.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common District Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them</h3>

  <p>Navigating the disciplinary process for students with disabilities can be challenging. Be aware of these common pitfalls and take steps to avoid them:</p>

  <ul>
      <li><b>Failure to conduct a timely MDR:</b> Ensure the school district conducts the MDR within the required timeframe (10 school days).</li>
      <li><b>Inadequate consideration of the student's IEP:</b> Make sure the IEP team thoroughly reviews the student's IEP and considers how the goals and accommodations relate to the behavior.</li>
      <li><b>Insufficient FBA and BIP:</b> Ensure the FBA is comprehensive and the BIP is tailored to the student's specific needs and addresses the underlying causes of the behavior.</li>
      <li><b>Lack of parent involvement:</b> Actively participate in all stages of the disciplinary process and advocate for your child's rights.</li>
      <li><b>Failure to provide appropriate services during suspension:</b> Ensure the school district provides adequate services to allow your child to continue to participate in the general education curriculum and progress toward their IEP goals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>

  <dl>
      <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between a suspension and an expulsion?</b></dt>
      <dd><b>A:</b> A suspension is a temporary removal of a student from school, while an expulsion is a permanent removal. Expulsions are rare and require a more formal process.</dd>

      <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behavior that occurs off school grounds?</b></dt>
      <dd><b>A:</b> In some cases, yes. If the off-campus behavior is related to the school environment (e.g., bullying that impacts the school day) or violates the school's Code of Conduct, the student may be subject to disciplinary action.</dd>

      <dt><b>Q: What if my child's behavior is related to an undiagnosed disability?</b></dt>
      <dd><b>A:</b> If you suspect your child's behavior may be related to an undiagnosed disability, request an evaluation from the school district. The evaluation will help determine if your child is eligible for special education services.</dd>

      <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the school's decision to suspend my child?</b></dt>
      <dd><b>A:</b> You have the right to appeal the school's decision. The specific appeal process is outlined in the Central Islip UFSD Code of Conduct. You also have the right to file a due process complaint if you believe the school violated your child's rights under IDEA.</dd>
  </dl>
</body>
